EXT. LIFEBOAT - OPEN SEA - DAWN

A battered orange lifeboat rides a long swell under a pearl sky. Six figures huddle under a torn canopy. Salt crusts every face.

SKIPPER BRODIE (50s, captain's cap, split lip) holds the tiller with both hands. ADAIR (30s, ship's mate, ripped oilskin) sits opposite, passing a ration tin from hand to hand. MEI (20s, stewardess, wrapped in a blanket) holds a thin plastic cup under a drip of rain from the canopy. TOLLIVER (60s, first-class passenger, tattered dinner jacket) stares at the horizon with his hands between his knees. SAMSON (40s, engineer, sunburned scalp) rows with a single splintered oar. NAN (12, ship's boy, enormous lifejacket) sits at the bow, a rope around her wrist.

ADAIR
Biscuit.

He hands the tin to Mei. She takes one, hands it on. Tolliver takes one without looking. Samson stops rowing, takes one, and chews loudly.

BRODIE
We'll hold this heading until noon.

SAMSON
(between bites)
And if the wind turns?

BRODIE
Then we turn with it.

Nan lifts her head. She points at the horizon with her free hand. A thin black smudge at the line of the sea.

NAN
Skipper.

BRODIE
I see it.

TOLLIVER
Smoke?

BRODIE
Or a cloud.

SAMSON
It's smoke.

ADAIR
It could be a trawler.

MEI
Or something burning.

Nobody speaks. The lifeboat rocks. Mei sets down her cup with care. Tolliver straightens his dinner jacket.

TOLLIVER
Skipper, I have a flare in my pocket.

BRODIE
You what?

TOLLIVER
I had it all night. I did not wish to waste it.

Brodie stares at him. Samson lets the oar slip from his hand and clatter in the bottom of the boat. Adair closes his eyes and takes a long breath through his nose.

ADAIR
(low, to Brodie)
Sir, I'll take it from him.

BRODIE
No. Leave him his dignity.

Tolliver draws the flare from his pocket, unscrews the cap, and strikes it against the gunwale. A red plume of smoke shoots up. The lifeboat glows crimson.

For a moment nothing happens. Then Nan begins to laugh, and Mei begins to cry, and Samson wraps an arm around the girl and presses her head against his shoulder. Brodie lets the tiller go and sits back.

BRODIE (CONT'D)
(barely audible)
There she is.

On the horizon, the smudge grows a mast.
